		<article>
			<h1>Ticket Urgency</h1>
			<p>
				<strong>The Ticket Urgency is a measure from 1 to 10 raking the urgency to reply to a ticket.</strong>
			</p>
			<p>
				All tickets with the status "Awaiting Agent" have an urgency level. The urgency level is the default method for sorting tickets in any filter within the DeskPRO Agent interface;
				and encourages agents to respond to tickets in urgency order instead of a one dimensional order such as the date the ticket was opened.
			</p>
			<p>
				Different organizations will set different criteria to set the Urgency of tickets. Typical examples include:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>How long the ticket has been open.</li>
				<li>The time the user has been waiting for a reply.</li>
				<li>The total time (through the life of the ticket) the user has been awaiting a reply.</li>
				<li>Some property of the ticket, for example a ticket submitted to the Security department may receive a higher Urgency than a less time sensitive Feature Request.</li>
				<li>Some property of a user or organization, for example a VIP user group with a support contract may receive higher Urgency on their tickets.</li>
				<li>Tickets where a defined SLA will shortly fail.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				You can specify rules to set the urgency of tickets using
			</p>
			<ol>
				<li>Triggers - the urgency is set when something happens to a ticket (it is created, a reply is added, a property is changed)</li>
				<li>Escalations - some amount of time has elapsed since something happened (e.g. 3 hours since the ticket was created)</li>
				<li>SLAs - you can change the urgency of a ticket is a ticket is a risk of SLA failure.</li>
			</ol>
		</article>

